<h2>Key Takeaways</h2>
<ul>
<li>Plain, fully cooked turkey meat without skin, bones or seasoning is the safest food to share, in small amounts.</li>
<li>Turkey skin, drippings, gravy and ham are fatty or salty and can trigger pancreatitis days after the meal.</li>
<li>Onions, garlic, grapes, raisins, xylitol, chocolate, alcohol and raw yeast dough are toxic.</li>
<li>Cooked bones, corn cobs and turkey twine can cause choking or blockages.</li>
<li>If your pet eats something harmful, call your vet or a pet poison line right away.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Thanksgiving Foods Dogs Can Eat: Quick Chart</h2>
<p>“Small amount” means a taste or a spoonful, served plain: no butter, salt, sauce, onion, garlic or spice. “Toxic” foods can poison pets; “Avoid” foods commonly cause stomach upsets, pancreatitis or blockages.</p>

