<h2>Which Main-Course Foods Are Safe to Share?</h2>

<h3>Can dogs eat Thanksgiving turkey?</h3>
<p>Yes, in small amounts. A few bites of fully cooked turkey breast or thigh, with skin, bones, gravy and seasoning removed, are a reasonable treat for most healthy dogs. The ASPCA advises that shared meat be boneless and cooked through, since raw or undercooked poultry can carry Salmonella.</p>

<h3>Can dogs eat turkey bones?</h3>
<p>No. Cooked turkey bones can splinter, get stuck and injure the gut. The U.S. Food and Drug Administration (FDA) warns that bones can lodge in the esophagus, stomach or windpipe and that sharp fragments cause internal injuries. Pet Poison Helpline adds the twine used to truss the turkey to the list of blockage risks, so bin the carcass and string together.</p>

<h3>Can dogs eat turkey skin?</h3>
<p>It is best not to. Turkey skin is mostly fat, usually coated in butter, salt and seasoning. Pet Poison Helpline lists turkey skin, gravy and meat drippings among fatty foods that are not poisonous in a chemical sense but can cause vomiting, diarrhea or pancreatitis in dogs.</p>

<h3>Can dogs eat Thanksgiving ham?</h3>
<p>Ham is a poor choice. Holiday hams are typically salt-cured, fatty and finished with a sugary glaze. The ASPCA notes that too much salt causes thirst, increased urination and electrolyte problems, and fatty scraps are a known pancreatitis trigger. Leftover turkey brine is worse: Pet Poison Helpline warns it can cause salt toxicosis in dogs and cats.</p>

<h3>Can dogs eat Thanksgiving stuffing or dressing?</h3>
<p>No. Whether you call it stuffing or dressing, most recipes contain onion, garlic, butter and herbs, and some add raisins. Onion and garlic belong to the Allium genus, which the ASPCA says can damage red blood cells and lead to anemia. Pet Poison Helpline calls stuffing several hazards in one dish.</p>

<h2>Are Thanksgiving Sides and Desserts Safe for Pets?</h2>

<h3>Can dogs eat Thanksgiving yams or sweet potatoes?</h3>
<p>Plain cooked sweet potato or yam, without butter, brown sugar, spices or marshmallows, is fine for most dogs and cats in small amounts. The ASPCA suggests mixing a little plain sweet potato, green beans and turkey into a pet’s usual dinner.</p>

<h3>Can dogs eat mashed potatoes?</h3>
<p>Only if they are plain. Boiled potato mashed without butter, cream, cheese, garlic or onion is fine as a spoonful. Holiday versions rarely qualify: Pet Poison Helpline points out that onion and garlic often hide in mashed and scalloped potatoes, and the ASPCA notes that dairy can cause diarrhea in pets.</p>

<h3>Can dogs eat pumpkin pie?</h3>
<p>It is best not to share it. Pumpkin pie combines sugar, fat, pastry and spices such as nutmeg. A lick is unlikely to be serious, but a slice can upset the stomach. Plain canned pumpkin with nothing added is the safer seasonal taste.</p>

<h2>Thanksgiving Foods Toxic to Dogs and Cats</h2>
<p>These Thanksgiving foods dangerous for dogs and cats can poison pets and should never be offered, even as a taste:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Onions and garlic.</strong> Allium species damage red blood cells. The ASPCA says cats are more susceptible than dogs. Powders in gravy, rubs and stock count.</li>
<li><strong>Grapes and raisins.</strong> Grapes, raisins, currants and sultanas can cause kidney injury in dogs, and Pet Poison Helpline says no safe amount is known.</li>
<li><strong>Xylitol.</strong> Xylitol is a sugar-free sweetener. Pet Poison Helpline warns it can drop a dog’s blood sugar within 10 to 15 minutes and cause liver failure. The FDA notes it appears in some peanut butters and baked goods.</li>
<li><strong>Chocolate.</strong> It contains theobromine and caffeine; the FDA notes baking chocolate is the most dangerous.</li>
<li><strong>Raw yeast dough.</strong> Unbaked dough keeps rising in the stomach while yeast produces ethanol, risking bloat, a twisted stomach (gastric dilatation-volvulus, or GDV) and alcohol poisoning.</li>
<li><strong>Nutmeg.</strong> Nutmeg contains myristicin. The ASPCA warns large amounts can cause vomiting, agitation, a fast heart rate and hallucinations.</li>
<li><strong>Alcohol and macadamia nuts.</strong> Alcohol causes vomiting, incoordination and breathing problems (FDA); macadamias can cause weakness, tremors and overheating in dogs (ASPCA).</li>
</ul>
<p>Our <a href="/tools/dog-toxic-foods-guide/">toxic foods for dogs guide</a> covers more ingredients in detail.</p>

<h2>Can Cats Eat Turkey?</h2>
<p>Yes. Cats are obligate carnivores, meaning they need nutrients found in animal tissue, so a few small pieces of plain, fully cooked turkey make a natural treat. Lean turkey breast is the best choice. Remove skin, bones and seasoning, and avoid raw turkey, which Cornell Feline Health Center advises against because of infection risk.</p>
<p>Keep portions small; the <a href="/tools/cat-calorie-calculator/">cat calorie calculator</a> shows how little room a cat’s diet has for extras.</p>

<h3>Thanksgiving foods toxic to cats</h3>
<p>The most important Thanksgiving foods toxic to cats are onion, garlic, chives and leeks, often hidden as powder in gravy, stuffing and stock. Chocolate, alcohol, raw dough and xylitol foods should also be kept away, rich dairy dishes may cause diarrhea, and lilies in table arrangements are dangerous. See the <a href="/tools/cat-toxic-foods-guide/">cat toxic foods guide</a> for more.</p>

<h2>Pancreatitis in Dogs After Thanksgiving</h2>
<p>Pancreatitis is inflammation of the pancreas, the organ that makes digestive enzymes. The Merck Veterinary Manual names dietary indiscretion, such as raiding a fatty meal, as a likely common risk factor in dogs, and Miniature Schnauzers are strongly overrepresented. Pet Poison Helpline notes signs may appear one to four days after the meal. Watch for:</p>
<ol>
<li>Repeated vomiting</li>
<li>Belly pain or a hunched posture</li>
<li>Diarrhea</li>
<li>Loss of appetite, lethargy or weakness</li>
</ol>
<p>In cats, the FDA says signs are subtler, often just reduced appetite and weight loss. Pancreatitis needs a veterinarian. Our <a href="/tools/cat-pancreatitis-guide/">cat pancreatitis guide</a> covers feline signs, and the <a href="/tools/pet-stomach-upset-guide/">pet stomach upset guide</a> helps you judge when vomiting needs urgent care.</p>

<h2>Can Dogs Eat Thanksgiving Leftovers?</h2>
<p>Only plain leftovers in small amounts. Turkey meat that was refrigerated promptly and is free of skin, bones and gravy is fine as a small treat in the following days. Leftover stuffing, gravy, ham, casseroles and desserts carry the same risks as on the day. The <a href="/tools/dog-treat-calculator/">dog treat calculator</a> shows how much fits into your dog’s day.</p>

<h2>How to Include Your Pet Safely</h2>

