<h2>What to Do If Your Pet Eats Something They Should Not</h2>
<p>Call before signs appear: xylitol and grapes can cause harm before a pet looks unwell.</p>
<ol>
<li>Move your pet away and remove what is left.</li>
<li>Note what was eaten, how much and when; keep the packaging or recipe.</li>
<li>Call your veterinarian or a poison line. In the United States, the ASPCA Animal Poison Control Center is (888) 426-4435 and Pet Poison Helpline is (855) 764-7661. A fee may apply.</li>
<li>Do not give home remedies or try to make your pet vomit; follow the vet’s instructions.</li>
<li>Find your nearest emergency clinic in advance, since your regular clinic may be closed on Thanksgiving.</li>
</ol>
<p>The <a href="/tools/pet-poison-emergency-guide/">pet poison emergency guide</a> covers these steps, and our article on <a href="/blog/signs-your-pet-is-sick/">signs your pet is sick</a> explains which symptoms are urgent.</p>

<h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

<h3>What Thanksgiving foods can dogs eat?</h3>
<p>Dogs can usually have small amounts of plain, fully cooked turkey without skin or bones, plain sweet potato, plain boiled potato, green beans, carrots and plain canned pumpkin. Serve everything without butter, salt, onion, garlic, gravy or spices, and keep portions small next to their normal food.</p>

<h3>Can cats eat turkey breast?</h3>
<p>Yes. Plain, fully cooked turkey breast is lean animal protein and one of the safest holiday treats for cats. Remove skin, bones and seasoning, cut it small and keep portions modest; Cornell Feline Health Center suggests treats stay within 10 to 15 percent of daily calories.</p>

<h3>Is gravy safe for dogs or cats?</h3>
<p>Gravy is best avoided. It is usually made from fatty pan drippings and often contains onion or garlic powder, salt and stock. Fatty foods are linked to pancreatitis, and Allium ingredients can damage red blood cells. A little plain turkey makes a safer treat.</p>

<h3>Can dogs eat cranberry sauce?</h3>
<p>It is not a good choice. Most cranberry sauce is high in sugar, and some recipes add raisins or alcohol, which are dangerous for dogs. A lick of plain sauce is unlikely to be an emergency, but it offers no benefit, so keep it off your dog’s plate.</p>

<h3>How long after eating fatty food can a dog get pancreatitis?</h3>
<p>Pet Poison Helpline notes that signs may not appear until one to four days after a fatty meal, so a dog who seemed fine on Thanksgiving night can become ill later in the weekend. Repeated vomiting, belly pain, diarrhea, loss of appetite or lethargy are reasons to call a vet.</p>

<h3>Is a little onion or garlic in stuffing a problem?</h3>
<p>It can be. Onion and garlic damage red blood cells, and the effect depends on the amount eaten relative to body size. The ASPCA says cats are more sensitive than dogs. Stuffing and gravy often use concentrated powders, so call your vet or a poison line if your pet eats a noticeable amount.</p>

<h3>Is nutmeg toxic to dogs?</h3>
<p>Nutmeg contains myristicin, and the ASPCA warns that large amounts can cause vomiting, agitation, a fast heart rate and hallucinations. The small amount in one slice of pie is not usually the main danger, but whole nutmegs or a spilled jar are. Call your vet if your dog eats a significant amount.</p>

<h3>What should I do if my dog ate raw bread dough?</h3>
<p>Call your veterinarian or a pet poison line right away. Raw yeast dough keeps expanding in the stomach and produces alcohol as it ferments, which can cause bloat, a twisted stomach and alcohol poisoning. Do not treat it at home. Signs include a swollen belly, unproductive retching and wobbliness.</p>

